@andreatramacere I think I understand what you mean, sorry about the confusion. You would like that when the user calls `DBSCAN` with the `haversine` metrics, the code did the spherical to cartesian conversion, and then used the angular distance (which essentially means that `NearestNeighbor` should be able to do that), right? This is problematic (independently of the precision issue) because while it could work with brute force neighbor search (an possibly  https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/pull/4458 might consider it), this becomes more complex with the KDTree and BallTree where the principle of using a base Tree class + a fast distance function in Cython wouldn't work anymore (and in 2D a tree based NN search would probably be faster). One would need also to be sure that it's the fastest approach for any possible use cases of NearestNeighbors with the `haversine` metric...

Essentially, the angular distance has the same issue, where it's much more efficient to  compute a L2 norm once on the data, and then compute the cosine distance as a dot product (instead to recomputing the normalization for each pair of points) but this would mean that the distance would not be valid for non L2 normalized data anymore.

Since scikit-learn aims to be a generic library, some performance aspects do get sacrificed to remain general. But in this case you can still manually do the coordinate conversion on your data before providing it to DBSCAN, if that's suitable for your application...

